        <section class="practice-section">
            <div class="activity-wrap">
                <header class="activity-section-header">
                    <div>
                        <span class="activity-eyebrow">The Work in Everyday Life</span>
                        <h2>Where knowledge becomes relationship and service</h2>
                    </div>
                    <p>These programmes are not isolated events. They extend the Mission’s relationship with students, seekers and families beyond a scholarship or a single moment of assistance.</p>
                </header>

                <div class="practice-list">
                    <article class="practice-item" id="sunday-sanskar">
                        <figure class="practice-figure">
                            <img src="assets/images/sanskar_classes.jpg" alt="Students gathered with mentors for Sunday Sanskar and personality development classes at Manidweep" loading="lazy">
                            <figcaption>Sunday morning at Manidweep—learning that reaches beyond textbooks.</figcaption>
                        </figure>
                        <div class="practice-copy">
                            <span class="practice-order">01 · Formation</span>
                            <h3>Sunday Sanskar &amp; Personality Development Classes</h3>
                            <p>Education can sharpen the intellect while leaving anger, fear, ego and self-doubt untouched. These Sunday sessions bring knowledge and Sanskar together, nurturing confidence, gratitude, discipline, compassion and awareness of both rights and responsibilities.</p>
                            <p>Students receive academic guidance, counselling, life-skills support and a peaceful space in which questions can be voiced without judgement. The aim is not simply examination success, but a balanced and self-reliant person.</p>
                            <div class="practice-meta"><span>Every Sunday, July–December</span><span>10:30 AM–12:30 PM</span><span>Open without discrimination</span></div>
                            <a class="practice-source" href="sunday-sanskar-classes.html">Explore Sunday Sanskar Classes</a>
                        </div>
                    </article>

                    <article class="practice-item is-reversed" id="sunday-evening-classes">
                        <span class="anchor-alias" id="holistic-wellness" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                        <figure class="practice-figure">
                            <img src="assets/images/sunday1.jpg" alt="Families and seekers gathered for a Sunday evening spiritual discussion at Manidweep" loading="lazy">
                            <figcaption>A shared space for spiritual inquiry, reflection and practical guidance.</figcaption>
                        </figure>
                        <div class="practice-copy">
                            <span class="practice-order">02 · Inner life</span>
                            <h3>Sunday Evening Classes for Everyone</h3>
                            <p>These gatherings welcome householders and seekers into an open exploration of the purpose of human life, the relationship between spirituality and material responsibility, and the practice of living peacefully within family and society.</p>
                            <p>Rooted in Bhaiyaji’s Buddhi-Vivek Yog Sadhna and carried forward by Maa Basanti and now Madhu Maa, the sessions connect spiritual understanding with real questions, relationships and challenges. Yoga, Pranayama and meditation support development across the physical, mental, spiritual and emotional dimensions.</p>
                            <div class="practice-meta"><span>Open to all seekers</span><span>Discussion and Sadhna</span><span>Holistic wellbeing</span></div>
                        </div>
                    </article>

                    <article class="practice-item" id="school-visits">
                        <figure class="practice-figure">
                            <img src="assets/images/girl_speech.jpg" alt="A student speaking confidently during a Mission mentoring programme" loading="lazy">
                            <figcaption>Confidence grows when students are trusted with a voice of their own.</figcaption>
                        </figure>
                        <div class="practice-copy">
                            <span class="practice-order">03 · Outreach</span>
                            <h3>School Visits</h3>
                            <p>The Mission reaches students in their own learning environments through motivational talks, value-based discussions, Yoga and Pranayama sessions, educational guidance and the distribution of constructive literature.</p>
                            <p>These visits also bring teachers and parents into the conversation. By listening to the whole ecosystem around a student, volunteers can identify barriers early and encourage families to see a daughter’s education as an investment in her dignity and future.</p>
                            <div class="practice-meta"><span>Motivational interaction</span><span>Student wellbeing</span><span>Parent and teacher engagement</span></div>
                        </div>
                    </article>

                    <article class="practice-item is-reversed" id="infrastructure-support">
                        <figure class="practice-figure">
                            <img src="assets/images/infra1.jpg" alt="Bhaiyaji and supporters visiting a school construction site" loading="lazy">
                            <figcaption>Bhaiyaji visiting a school construction site—support extending beyond fees.</figcaption>
                        </figure>
                        <div class="practice-copy">
                            <span class="practice-order">04 · Learning environments</span>
                            <h3>Infrastructure Support</h3>
                            <p>Bhaiyaji recognised that a scholarship could not fully serve a student expected to learn without safe classrooms, drinking water, toilets, furniture or secure school boundaries. He contributed personally and inspired public representatives and fellow members to help address these conditions.</p>
                            <p>Over time, support included classrooms, halls, computer laboratories, water-huts, sanitation facilities, boundary walls and furniture across several Jodhpur schools—creating environments in which education could be pursued with greater safety and dignity.</p>
                            <div class="practice-meta"><span>Classrooms and halls</span><span>Water and sanitation</span><span>Furniture and computer labs</span></div>
                            <a class="practice-source" href="infrastructure-support.html">Explore Infrastructure Support</a>
                        </div>
                    </article>

                    <article class="practice-item" id="family-support">
                        <figure class="practice-figure">
                            <img src="assets/images/ration.jpg" alt="Madhu Maa and Mission volunteers providing ration support to a family" loading="lazy">
                            <figcaption>Essential support offered with continuity, respect and personal connection.</figcaption>
                        </figure>
                        <div class="practice-copy">
                            <span class="practice-order">05 · Family dignity</span>
                            <h3>Ration &amp; Family Support</h3>
                            <p>During the pandemic, conversations with students revealed widowed mothers, parents unable to work through illness and families being forced to choose between rent, food and education. The Mission responded because a child’s learning cannot remain secure while the household around her is in crisis.</p>
                            <p>What began as emergency ration assistance became a continuing circle of care. Support is offered with dignity and reviewed with sensitivity; families who regain stability voluntarily make room for others facing greater need.</p>
                            <div class="practice-meta"><span>Begun October 2020</span><span>Essential food support</span><span>Dignity-first approach</span></div>
                        </div>
                    </article>

                    <article class="practice-item is-reversed" id="sewing-centre">
                        <div class="activity-visual-textile" role="img" aria-label="A decorative representation of skill growing into dignity and independence">
                            <span>Skill becomes confidence. Confidence becomes freedom.</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="practice-copy">
                            <span class="practice-order">06 · Self-reliance</span>
                            <h3>Vatsalyamayi Maa Basanti Ji Manihar Sewing Centre</h3>
                            <p>Free instruction in sewing, tailoring and garment construction gives women and girls an accessible skill that can support household income and personal independence. The training is practical, but its deeper purpose is confidence—the knowledge that one can create, earn and stand with dignity.</p>
                            <p>Named in Maa Basanti’s loving memory, the Centre carries forward her conviction that education must be joined with the ability to become self-reliant.</p>
                            <div class="practice-meta"><span>Established 2025</span><span>Free vocational training</span><span>Women and girls</span></div>
                        </div>
                    </article>
                </div>
            </div>
